$DIR/main.p:17:20: channel element type Job is not sendable: it contains shared[int], whose count is not atomic, so it cannot cross puroutines; use synced[T] (spec section 4.7)
    A value that crosses puroutines, as the argument or receiver of a go
    statement or as the element of a channel, must be sendable. This type holds
    a shared[T], whose reference count is changed with ordinary arithmetic, so
    two puroutines copying or dropping references at once would corrupt it.
    Use synced[T], which counts atomically.
    Go's garbage collector makes every pointer safe to share. Plutonium counts
    shared references without atomic instructions, which is only correct on one
    puroutine.
    Count the references atomically:
        type Job struct {
            data synced[Buffer]
        }

        jobs := make(chan Job, 16)
    (see "go starts a puroutine" in the Plutonium spec, section 4.7)
    (run "pluto explain E2010" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:21:10: &x is passed to a go statement, but x lives in this function's frame, which the receiving puroutine may outlive (spec section 6.3); allocate it with new, whose owned[T] then moves to the puroutine, or make it a package-level variable
    This value points into the current function's stack frame: the address of a
    local variable or of part of one, a slice of a local array, or the address
    of a composite literal. A go statement or a channel send hands it to another
    puroutine, which may still use it after this function has returned and its
    frame is gone.
    Allocate the variable with new, so that it outlives the frame, or make it a
    package-level variable.
    Go moves such variables to the heap without saying so. Plutonium never
    allocates behind the program's back, so a local stays in its frame.
    Allocate it, then pass the pointer:
        wg := new(sync.WaitGroup)
        wg.Add(1)
        go worker(wg)
        wg.Wait()
        alloc.Free(wg)
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 6.3)
    (run "pluto explain E2011" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:24:9: buf[:] is passed to a go statement, but it is a slice of buf, which lives in this function's frame and which the receiving puroutine may outlive (spec section 6.3)
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2011" for more)

$DIR/main.p:27:5: go statement calls a pointer method on c, a variable in this function's frame, which the puroutine may outlive (spec section 6.3); make it a package-level variable, or give the puroutine a function that takes an owner
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2011" for more)

$DIR/main.p:30:10: &x is sent on a channel, but x lives in this function's frame, which the receiving puroutine may outlive (spec section 6.3); allocate it with new, whose owned[T] then moves to the puroutine, or make it a package-level variable
    (explained above; run "pluto explain E2011" for more)
